<li>Mirror Lake: 119 days&nbsp;</li>
<li>Lake Neusiedl: 104 days</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul>
</li>
</ul></div>
<p>It is common for freshwater lakes near or above a latitude of 45&deg; north of the equator, like Lake Mj&oslash;sa in Norway, to accumulate surface ice in winter.&nbsp; The amount and duration of ice depends on many factors, including local weather conditions as well as the lake&rsquo;s depth, volume, and surface area, but a climate researcher claims that some lakes in these latitudes have seen a decline in the duration of ice between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s.&nbsp; She cites as a typical example <span aria-hidden="true">______</span><span class="sr-only">blank</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the researcher&rsquo;s example?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"be82f1b7-0066-422b-aee1-e8c6b45c20ea","content":"<p>both Lake Neusiedl and Ouluj&auml;rvi, which had fewer than 195 days of ice in the winter of 1980&ndash;81.</p>"},{"id":"13177abd-0511-4ca6-a3de-b9df04cfc055","content":"<p>Lake Neusiedl, which had more days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06 than it did in the winter of 1980&ndash;81.</p>"},{"id":"09b90fa0-caac-4aa1-b995-e8addd67ac52","content":"<p>Ouluj&auml;rvi, which had fewer days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06 than it did in the winter of 1980&ndash;81.</p>"},{"id":"19cea203-ea65-4a27-ac1c-e6ff6dff6dcd","content":"<p>both Lake Neusiedl and Ouluj&auml;rvi, which had more than 105 days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["C"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["09b90fa0-caac-4aa1-b995-e8addd67ac52"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to exemplify the researcher&rsquo;s claim&mdash;namely, that the duration of ice on some lakes has declined between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s. According to the graph, Ouluj&auml;rvi had surface ice for nearly 200 days in the winter of 1980&ndash;81 but only about 160 days of ice in the more recent winter of 2005&ndash;06, evidence of a clear decline in the duration of surface ice between these time periods.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it focuses on only one period of time (the early 1980s). Thus, the data it cites don&rsquo;t support the researcher&rsquo;s claim, which compares the duration of surface ice across two specific periods of time (the early 1980s and the mid-2000s). Choice B is incorrect because although it accurately describes data from the graph, it contradicts the researcher&rsquo;s claim about declining surface ice duration. It describes an increase, not a decline, in the duration of surface ice on Lake Neusiedl between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s (from about 80 days in 1980&ndash;81 to about 100 days in 2005&ndash;06). Therefore, the example of Lake Neusiedl wouldn&rsquo;t logically support the claim that some lakes have seen a decline in the duration of surface ice. Choice D is incorrect because it provides information that doesn&rsquo;t support the researcher&rsquo;s claim about declining ice duration. The graph could indicate that both Lake Neusiedl and Ouluj&auml;rvi had more than 105 days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06, but on its own, this information doesn&rsquo;t demonstrate a decline in ice duration between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d74b9bc6","external_id":"295bc087-4e63-4ec3-a24a-fc0bde6599d7","vaultid":null,"uId":"6d85a516-6dba-4547-870d-bca4fbb5cac0","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":5,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<figure class=\"image\"><svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" width=\"400\" height=\"498.53955078125\" viewBox=\"0 0 400 498.53955078125\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Bar graph titled Days per Winter That Lakes Have Surface Ice. The horizontal axis is labeled Winter. 2 data categories are shown. The vertical axis is labeled Days. It ranges from 0 to 200 in increments of 40. Refer to long description.\"><g id=\"ed420550-79eb-48d4-af01-cd27cdd08afd\" data-name=\"Layer 1\"><defs>\n      <pattern id=\"bar4\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"10\" height=\"100\" patternUnits=\"userSpaceOnUse\" patternTransform=\"rotate(50)\">\n        <rect x=\"0\" y=\"0\" width=\"5\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#CDCDCD\"/>\n        <rect x=\"5\" y=\"0\" width=\"5\" height=\"100\" fill=\"#444444\"/>\n      </pattern>\n    </defs><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"83.83998680114746\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"95.83998680114746\" y2=\"72\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(77.83998680114746 78)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">200</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"112\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"83.83998680114746\" y1=\"112\" x2=\"95.83998680114746\" y2=\"112\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(77.83998680114746 118)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">160</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"152\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"152\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"83.83998680114746\" y1=\"152\" x2=\"95.83998680114746\" y2=\"152\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(77.83998680114746 158)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">120</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"192\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"192\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"83.83998680114746\" y1=\"192\" x2=\"95.83998680114746\" y2=\"192\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(77.83998680114746 198)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">80</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"232\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"232\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"83.83998680114746\" y1=\"232\" x2=\"95.83998680114746\" y2=\"232\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(77.83998680114746 238)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">40</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"272\" x2=\"385\" y2=\"272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"83.83998680114746\" y1=\"272\" x2=\"95.83998680114746\" y2=\"272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(77.83998680114746 278)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">0</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(24 172) rotate(-90)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Days</text><line xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x1=\"89.83998680114746\" y1=\"72\" x2=\"89.83998680114746\" y2=\"272\" fill=\"none\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"round\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"122.63554382324219\" y=\"78\" height=\"194\" width=\"32.79555702209473\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"155.4311008453369\" y=\"150\" height=\"122\" width=\"32.79555702209473\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"188.22665786743164\" y=\"195\" height=\"77\" width=\"32.79555702209473\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(181.74887935638426 291.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">1980&ndash;81</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"253.8177719116211\" y=\"117\" height=\"155\" width=\"32.79555702209473\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"286.6133289337158\" y=\"153\" height=\"119\" width=\"32.79555702209473\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"319.40888595581055\" y=\"168\" height=\"104\" width=\"32.79555702209473\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linecap=\"round\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"0\" y=\"0\" text-anchor=\"end\" transform=\"translate(312.9311074447632 291.84) rotate(-40)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">2005&ndash;06</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(237.41999340057373 24)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Days per Winter That Lakes Have</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(237.41999340057373 48)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Surface Ice</text><text text-anchor=\"middle\" transform=\"translate(237.41999340057373 367.37955078125)\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\">Winter</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" fill=\"none\" x=\"129.92708206176758\" y=\"384.53955078125\" height=\"103\" width=\"155.14583587646484\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"136.92708206176758\" y=\"396.53955078125\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#666666\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(158.92708206176758 408.53955078125)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> Ouluj&auml;rvi</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"136.92708206176758\" y=\"428.53955078125\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#CCCCCC\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(158.92708206176758 440.53955078125)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> Mirror Lake</text><rect xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" x=\"136.92708206176758\" y=\"460.53955078125\" height=\"12\" width=\"12\" fill=\"#000000\" stroke=\"#000000\" stroke-linejoin=\"mitre\" stroke-width=\"0.9\"/><text transform=\"translate(158.92708206176758 472.53955078125)\" text-anchor=\"top\" fill=\"#000000\" font-size=\"19.84\" font-family=\"Crimson Text\"> Lake Neusiedl</text></g></svg></figure><div class=\"sr-only\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Long description for bar graph titled Days per Winter That Lakes Have Surface Ice\"><ul>\n<li>For each data category, the following bars are shown:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Ouluj&auml;rvi</li>\n<li>Mirror Lake</li>\n<li>Lake Neusiedl</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>The data for the 2 categories are as follows:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>1980&ndash;81:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Ouluj&auml;rvi: 194 days</li>\n<li>Mirror Lake: 122 days</li>\n<li>Lake Neusiedl: 77 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>2005&ndash;06:<br/>\n<ul>\n<li>Ouluj&auml;rvi: 155 days</li>\n<li>Mirror Lake: 119 days&nbsp;</li>\n<li>Lake Neusiedl: 104 days</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ul></div>\n<p>It is common for freshwater lakes near or above a latitude of 45&deg; north of the equator, like Lake Mj&oslash;sa in Norway, to accumulate surface ice in winter.&nbsp; The amount and duration of ice depends on many factors, including local weather conditions as well as the lake&rsquo;s depth, volume, and surface area, but a climate researcher claims that some lakes in these latitudes have seen a decline in the duration of ice between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s.&nbsp; She cites as a typical example <span aria-hidden=\"true\">______</span><span class=\"sr-only\">blank</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the researcher&rsquo;s example?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"be82f1b7-0066-422b-aee1-e8c6b45c20ea","content":"<p>both Lake Neusiedl and Ouluj&auml;rvi, which had fewer than 195 days of ice in the winter of 1980&ndash;81.</p>"},{"id":"13177abd-0511-4ca6-a3de-b9df04cfc055","content":"<p>Lake Neusiedl, which had more days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06 than it did in the winter of 1980&ndash;81.</p>"},{"id":"09b90fa0-caac-4aa1-b995-e8addd67ac52","content":"<p>Ouluj&auml;rvi, which had fewer days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06 than it did in the winter of 1980&ndash;81.</p>"},{"id":"19cea203-ea65-4a27-ac1c-e6ff6dff6dcd","content":"<p>both Lake Neusiedl and Ouluj&auml;rvi, which had more than 105 days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06.</p>"}],"keys":["09b90fa0-caac-4aa1-b995-e8addd67ac52"],"correct_answer":["C"],"rationale":"<p>Choice C is the best answer because it most effectively uses data from the graph to exemplify the researcher&rsquo;s claim&mdash;namely, that the duration of ice on some lakes has declined between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s. According to the graph, Ouluj&auml;rvi had surface ice for nearly 200 days in the winter of 1980&ndash;81 but only about 160 days of ice in the more recent winter of 2005&ndash;06, evidence of a clear decline in the duration of surface ice between these time periods.</p><p>Choice A is incorrect because it focuses on only one period of time (the early 1980s). Thus, the data it cites don&rsquo;t support the researcher&rsquo;s claim, which compares the duration of surface ice across two specific periods of time (the early 1980s and the mid-2000s). Choice B is incorrect because although it accurately describes data from the graph, it contradicts the researcher&rsquo;s claim about declining surface ice duration. It describes an increase, not a decline, in the duration of surface ice on Lake Neusiedl between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s (from about 80 days in 1980&ndash;81 to about 100 days in 2005&ndash;06). Therefore, the example of Lake Neusiedl wouldn&rsquo;t logically support the claim that some lakes have seen a decline in the duration of surface ice. Choice D is incorrect because it provides information that doesn&rsquo;t support the researcher&rsquo;s claim about declining ice duration. The graph could indicate that both Lake Neusiedl and Ouluj&auml;rvi had more than 105 days of ice in the winter of 2005&ndash;06, but on its own, this information doesn&rsquo;t demonstrate a decline in ice duration between the early 1980s and the mid-2000s.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1755117061176,"updateDate":1755117061176}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1755117061176, 1755117061176),
    ($SATQ$d76ea360$SATQ$, $SATQ$33334091-eb57-44af-95a6-fbc105389a91$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Puerto Rico is an island in the Caribbean Sea. Indigenous people there started raising guinea pigs about 1,700 years ago. Guinea pigs had originally been domesticated much earlier in both Colombia and Peru. So were guinea pigs brought to Puerto Rico from Colombia or from Peru? Ancient Caribbean trade routes connected Puerto Rico with Colombia but not with Peru. <span style="text-decoration: underline;" role="region" aria-label="Referenced Content">Therefore, guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs.</span></p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the underlined claim?</p>$SATQ$, $SATQ$[{"id":"466c2e24-c9a3-40d8-a6cd-9359083c2ff2","content":"<p>Ancient guinea pigs in Puerto Rico were genetically less similar to ancient guinea pigs in Colombia than to ancient guinea pigs in Peru.</p>"},{"id":"2771199b-c50a-4735-8963-e40e3d024846","content":"<p>Guinea pigs are common in ancient Puerto Rican art, especially in pottery.</p>"},{"id":"0ce92f8d-2778-4451-a408-65aa71f20c74","content":"<p>Modern breeds of guinea pigs don&rsquo;t look like images of guinea pigs in ancient art from Puerto Rico, Colombia, and Peru.</p>"},{"id":"94fa1105-3e14-40a0-ad49-be5ffc842109","content":"<p>The guinea pig population of ancient Colombia was much larger than the guinea pig population of ancient Peru.</p>"}]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["A"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$["466c2e24-c9a3-40d8-a6cd-9359083c2ff2"]$SATQ$::jsonb, $SATQ$<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly weaken the underlined claim that guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs. The text makes this claim based on the fact that ancient trade routes connected Puerto Rico with Colombia but not with Peru. However, if it&rsquo;s true that ancient guinea pigs in Puerto Rico were genetically less similar to ancient guinea pigs in Colombia than to ancient guinea pigs in Peru, this would suggest that the Puerto Rican guinea pigs didn&rsquo;t descend from Colombian guinea pigs. Instead, Puerto Rican guinea pigs&rsquo; greater genetic similarity to Peruvian guinea pigs implies that ancient guinea pigs in Puerto Rico were likely brought to the island from Peru, despite the lack of direct ancient trade routes between the two regions.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While a finding about how common guinea pig imagery is in ancient Puerto Rican art would indicate the cultural significance of guinea pigs, it wouldn&rsquo;t be relevant to a claim about the geographical origin or descent of the guinea pig population on the island. The guinea pigs depicted in the artwork could have descended from guinea pigs in either Colombia or Peru; therefore, the finding would neither weaken nor strengthen the claim that guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs. Choice C is incorrect because the finding that modern breeds of guinea pigs don&rsquo;t look like images of guinea pigs in ancient art from Puerto Rico, Colombia, and Peru wouldn&rsquo;t address the ancestral relationships between ancient guinea pig populations in these three regions. Therefore, the finding would neither weaken nor strengthen the claim that guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs. Choice D is incorrect because a finding about the population sizes of guinea pigs in ancient Colombia and ancient Peru wouldn&rsquo;t be relevant to a claim about which population the Puerto Rican guinea pigs likely descended from. The smaller guinea pig population of Peru could still have been the source for the Puerto Rican guinea pig population.</p>$SATQ$, false, 0, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, NULL, $SATQ${"questionId":"d76ea360","external_id":"33334091-eb57-44af-95a6-fbc105389a91","vaultid":null,"uId":"c695fdf3-4c04-4679-a4c6-cd3c8c24e7d6","program":"SAT","test_type":1,"test_type_desc":"Reading and Writing","primary_class_cd":"INI","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill_cd":"COE","skill_desc":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"M","score_band_range_cd":4,"type":"mcq","stimulus":"<p>Puerto Rico is an island in the Caribbean Sea. Indigenous people there started raising guinea pigs about 1,700 years ago. Guinea pigs had originally been domesticated much earlier in both Colombia and Peru. So were guinea pigs brought to Puerto Rico from Colombia or from Peru? Ancient Caribbean trade routes connected Puerto Rico with Colombia but not with Peru. <span style=\"text-decoration: underline;\" role=\"region\" aria-label=\"Referenced Content\">Therefore, guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs.</span></p>","stem":"<p>Which finding, if true, would most directly weaken the underlined claim?</p>","answerOptions":[{"id":"466c2e24-c9a3-40d8-a6cd-9359083c2ff2","content":"<p>Ancient guinea pigs in Puerto Rico were genetically less similar to ancient guinea pigs in Colombia than to ancient guinea pigs in Peru.</p>"},{"id":"2771199b-c50a-4735-8963-e40e3d024846","content":"<p>Guinea pigs are common in ancient Puerto Rican art, especially in pottery.</p>"},{"id":"0ce92f8d-2778-4451-a408-65aa71f20c74","content":"<p>Modern breeds of guinea pigs don&rsquo;t look like images of guinea pigs in ancient art from Puerto Rico, Colombia, and Peru.</p>"},{"id":"94fa1105-3e14-40a0-ad49-be5ffc842109","content":"<p>The guinea pig population of ancient Colombia was much larger than the guinea pig population of ancient Peru.</p>"}],"keys":["466c2e24-c9a3-40d8-a6cd-9359083c2ff2"],"correct_answer":["A"],"rationale":"<p>Choice A is the best answer because it presents a finding that, if true, would most directly weaken the underlined claim that guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs. The text makes this claim based on the fact that ancient trade routes connected Puerto Rico with Colombia but not with Peru. However, if it&rsquo;s true that ancient guinea pigs in Puerto Rico were genetically less similar to ancient guinea pigs in Colombia than to ancient guinea pigs in Peru, this would suggest that the Puerto Rican guinea pigs didn&rsquo;t descend from Colombian guinea pigs. Instead, Puerto Rican guinea pigs&rsquo; greater genetic similarity to Peruvian guinea pigs implies that ancient guinea pigs in Puerto Rico were likely brought to the island from Peru, despite the lack of direct ancient trade routes between the two regions.</p><p>Choice B is incorrect. While a finding about how common guinea pig imagery is in ancient Puerto Rican art would indicate the cultural significance of guinea pigs, it wouldn&rsquo;t be relevant to a claim about the geographical origin or descent of the guinea pig population on the island. The guinea pigs depicted in the artwork could have descended from guinea pigs in either Colombia or Peru; therefore, the finding would neither weaken nor strengthen the claim that guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs. Choice C is incorrect because the finding that modern breeds of guinea pigs don&rsquo;t look like images of guinea pigs in ancient art from Puerto Rico, Colombia, and Peru wouldn&rsquo;t address the ancestral relationships between ancient guinea pig populations in these three regions. Therefore, the finding would neither weaken nor strengthen the claim that guinea pigs in Puerto Rico probably came from Colombia and descended from Colombian guinea pigs. Choice D is incorrect because a finding about the population sizes of guinea pigs in ancient Colombia and ancient Peru wouldn&rsquo;t be relevant to a claim about which population the Puerto Rican guinea pigs likely descended from. The smaller guinea pig population of Peru could still have been the source for the Puerto Rican guinea pig population.</p>","parenttemplatename":null,"parenttemplateid":null,"templateclusterid":null,"templateclustername":null,"origin":null,"position":null,"createDate":1743430669205,"updateDate":1743430669205}$SATQ$::jsonb, 1743430669205, 1743430669205),
    ($SATQ$d83c3d54$SATQ$, $SATQ$55af0cb2-1f16-4184-a04f-f39d41958743$SATQ$::uuid, $SATQ$9a061f4f-331a-40fb-9a95-94853963c6c8$SATQ$::uuid, NULL, $SATQ$qbank$SATQ$, $SATQ$SAT$SATQ$, 1, $SATQ$Reading and Writing$SATQ$, $SATQ$Information and Ideas$SATQ$, $SATQ$INI$SATQ$, $SATQ$COE$SATQ$, $SATQ$Command of Evidence$SATQ$, $SATQ$M$SATQ$, 4, $SATQ$mcq$SATQ$, $SATQ$<figure class="image"><svg aria-label="Bar graph titled Characteristics of the Banks of the Provo River Downstream of the Jordanelle Dam. The horizontal axis is labeled Year. 3 data categories are shown. The vertical axis is labeled Area, in square meters. It ranges from 0 to 140,000 in increments of 20,000. Refer to long description." height="480.84" role="img" viewbox="0 0 400 480.84" width="400" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg"><g data-name="Layer 1" id="ed420550-79eb-48d4-af01-cd27cdd08afd"><defs>
